<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Liberation Front (Algeria)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Front de Libération Nationale, FLN</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> (FLN) is an Algerian nationalist party founded in 1954. On 1 November 1954 it launched the armed struggle that became the Algerian War of Independence against French rule, fought by its army, the ALN, and by urban networks. After independence in 1962 it governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> as the sole legal party until multi-party politics was introduced in 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-algeria.jpg" alt="National Liberation Front (Algeria)" width="320" height="304" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National Liberation Front (Algeria) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:FLN_Logo.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Front grew out of Algerian nationalist sentiment that rose with the outbreak of WWII. When the Movement for the Triumph of Democratic Liberties, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messali-hadj">Messali Hadj</a>, was limited by a double electoral college that gave French settlers and the larger Muslim community equal representation of 60 seats each, some members turned towards a military approach through its paramilitary Special Organisation, whose figures included Ahmed Ben Bella, Hocine Aït Ahmed and Mohammed Boudiaf.</p><p>After the capture of Ben Bella in 1951 and the dismantling of that body, its militants formed the Revolutionary Committee of Unity and Action. It was succeeded by the Front, established on 10 October 1954. The Declaration of 1 November 1954, written by the journalist Mohamed Aïchaoui, opened the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By 1956 nearly all nationalist organisations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> had joined the Front, which had established itself as the main nationalist group by co-opting and coercing smaller ones. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messali-hadj">Messali Hadj</a>'s Algerian National Movement remained outside. The Front organised itself like a provisional government and divided the country into six wilayas, following Ottoman-era boundaries. Its National Liberation Army fought partly as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> units inside Algeria and partly as a stronger, more conventional force based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, later led by Colonel Houari Boumédiène. The war killed between 300,000 and 1.5 million people. The Front is held responsible for over 16,000 civilian deaths, and is thought to have massacred thousands of harkis after the ceasefire of 19 March 1962.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evian-accords">Évian Accords</a>, a power struggle split the leadership. Boumédiène's army put down resistance and installed Ben Bella as president, who outlawed opposition parties and purged dissidents such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferhat-abbas">Ferhat Abbas</a>. In 1965 Boumédiène overthrew him, ruling by decree and marginalising the party. After his death in 1978, Colonel Chadli Bendjedid succeeded him, and riots in 1988 led to an amended constitution that allowed several parties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Front drew its legitimacy from three sources: nationalism and the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, a loosely interpreted socialism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> as the foundation of national identity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frantz-fanon">Frantz Fanon</a> was a member and influenced its ideology during the struggle. The Constitution of October 1963 declared Islam the state religion and Arabic the sole national language, with Arabization as a priority.</p><p>Its stress on a single Arab and Muslim identity led it to refuse the separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> identity of the roughly 20% of Algerians who are Berbers. Hocine Aït Ahmed founded the Socialist Forces Front and began a rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabylia">Kabylia</a> that the government defeated in 1965. Between 1961 and 1962 about 130,000 of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>'s 140,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> left, mostly for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Its role as liberator of the country has remained the core of its self-image.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National Liberation Front (Algeria)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war of independence from 1954 eventually tied down some 400,000 or more French troops, many of them conscripts, against the <b>FLN</b>’s National Liberation Army, and saw the fortified and electrified Morice Line built along the Tunisian border.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-algeria">French Algeria · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Algerian War began in 1954 with attacks by the <b>Front de Libération Nationale</b> (FLN) and ended with the Évian Accords of March 1962 and a referendum in which Algerians overwhelmingly voted for independence.</blockquote><a href="/state/algeria">Algeria</a></li></ul></section>
